` with your own CARGO_TARGET_DIR under /Users/gabe/Projects/caliper-fixtures/oracle-run/ (rust 1.99.1 first on PATH: /Users/gabe/.rustup/toolchains/1.99.1-aarch64-apple-darwin/bin), and also `pnpm install --offline --ignore-scripts --frozen-lockfile` where the oracle needs it. Output dir: /Users/gabe/Projects/caliper-fixtures/oracle-run/ (never /tmp). 2) The machine must be QUIET for timings: check `uptime` before each timed step; other agents may be building: if the 1-minute load average is above ~4, wait (use background commands, no foreground sleeps over the limit) and record the load next to each timing; say so plainly if you could not get a quiet machine. Run timed steps sequentially, never in parallel. 3) The weekly record: `scripts/weekly.py --bin <release binary> --oracle --runs 2` (adapt flags to its real usage; `--runs` semantics: read it) with --out/--markdown into the output dir, over all five corpora, with the checker on (the default). That gives wall time and peak RSS per tool (caliper, RDT, react-docgen), recall vs RDT, the oracle's contradiction rates, and the headline (how many props of the components caliper reports are right). Then budgets: re-run or reuse `--enforce-budgets` logic to state pass/fail per corpus against scripts/headline.py BUDGETS (chakra 18 s/14336 MiB, mantine 4 s/2048, antd 6 s/2048, shadcn 5.6 s/2048, spectrum 6.8 s/2048). 4) FIRST vs SECOND run explicitly, per corpus, for caliper alone: first = empty --cache-dir (cold), second = same command again with the cache kept; record wall time and peak RSS (`/usr/bin/time -l`), the checker on; also the `--no-checker` structural time for the same corpus (so we see the structural floor); and the ratio vs RDT's wall time (RDT has no cache: its first and only run). Take 3 runs of each and report the median; report the output size (canonical JSON bytes). 5) QUALITY: from the oracle/headline: per corpus, share of props right, wrong, not judged; recall vs RDT (components and props, split @types/react vs the rest); the number of components and props caliper reports with the default addon-style collapse (`--collapse-inherited-props dependencies`) vs none: median props per component on Chakra (was 845 -> 139 after collapse) and Button's inherited layers/argTypes (~146). Compare every number to the 0.11.0 numbers you can find in the earlier reports (final-perf, release-check) and to the latest weekly record if any is stored (look in /Users/gabe/Projects/caliper-fixtures/ and `gh run list --workflow weekly.yml` artifacts: skip downloading if gh is blocked). 6) STORYBOOK DEV MODE: with the demo harness in live-demo/ (or reproduce it: a Storybook dev server using the built addon package from this SHA over Chakra's stories or the corpus the demo used; the napi binding must be built from this SHA: `pnpm --filter caliper-docgen-napi build` or the repo's recipe), measure in a real browser (playwright MCP tools are available to you; load them with ToolSearch `select:mcp__playwright__browser_navigate,mcp__playwright__browser_evaluate,mcp__playwright__browser_wait_for,mcp__playwright__browser_take_screenshot,mcp__playwright__browser_close` as needed): (i) server cold start to first story render; (ii) time from opening a component's docs/Controls page to the first Controls (structural answer) and to the checker's upgraded answer, cold server and warm (second visit / second server start with caches kept); (iii) navigating to a neighbouring story (prefetched by the scheduler) vs a distant one; (iv) editing a component file's props while the server runs: time to the updated Controls; (v) memory (RSS) of the dev server process after the above. Use the same component the earlier demo used (Chakra ButtonProps) plus one big (Mantine or antd) and one small (shadcn). Compare with the earlier demo numbers (structural ~4-5 ms after ~65 ms start; checker first answer 125-130 ms warm; live upgrade ~154 ms) and, if feasible in the time left, with react-docgen-typescript's own dev-server cost on the same component (skip if it needs more than a few calls; say so). Check that the Controls show the right props: screenshot Chakra Button and one DOM component; verify argTypes count (~146 for Button) and that collapsed layers do not appear as rows. 7) Write /Users/gabe/Projects/caliper-fixtures/oracle-run/REPORT.md: a short summary first (5 bullets: performance first run, second run, quality, Storybook, what is worse than before), then tables per corpus (first/second/structural/RDT/ratio/RSS/size), quality table (right/wrong/not judged, recall), Storybook table with timings, a 'vs 0.11.0' section, 'what failed or was not measured' (be honest), and the exact commands and SHA and load averages used so a reader can repeat it. No marketing language; numbers with units. Do not change repo files, do not push; if something is broken (a gate fails, a corpus run dies, memory over budget) report it with the log tail rather than fixing it. Cap 130 tool calls; run long commands in the background and poll with short checks. Final message at most 90 words: SHA, the 3 headline numbers (Chakra first and second run wall/RSS, headline right-rate, Storybook checker-answer time), and the report path."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2618,"cache_read_input_tokens":715236,"output_tokens":3673,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":918},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2618,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3673,"cache_read_input_tokens":715236,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2618,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2618},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":