{"parentUuid":"c5555442-4d72-4a43-9426-dae05b78ce67","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch74HucTi8dWQCso9vxqP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2681,"cache_read_input_tokens":844070,"output_tokens":2494,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1042},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2681,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2494,"cache_read_input_tokens":844070,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2681,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2681},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":14006,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"cbc0c0ca-cbbc-4351-8843-f3d915bd835a","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch74HuFdM868YVKQA26mi","requestRef":"f483b1e2-f840-4e35-8920-45cd26ab49b1","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"62aeac96-a0a5-4be5-a1d9-bbea2fe72b3b","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:11:19.067Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-abdd35c983e9e5db8","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"mf-t4","slug":"hazy-conjuring-volcano"}
{"parentUuid":"62aeac96-a0a5-4be5-a1d9-bbea2fe72b3b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch74HucTi8dWQCso9vxqP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, there are levers. The first thing to know is that we already root the checker's program only at the files of the exported components, not the whole project. What's large is the transitive closure of everything those files import. From the code and docs, the options are:\n\n1. **`types`.** Our default compiler options (`jsx, module, moduleResolution, target, strict, skipLibCheck, noEmit`) set no `types` list. Without one, TypeScript pulls in every `node_modules/@types/*` package it can see (node, jest, and so on), whether or not a component uses them. Naming only what's needed, such as `react` and `react-dom`, could drop a lot of declaration memory. The risk is a component that relies on a global from another `@types` package, so it needs a fallback when a project's tsconfig sets its own `types`.\n2. **Several smaller programs, one at a time.** Split the component files into batches, create a program for each, convert the answers, and free it before the next. Peak memory becomes the largest batch's closure instead of the whole project's. The cost is re-reading shared declarations such as React's, so it trades time for memory. It also only helps the one-shot CLI. The dev server keeps one program alive.\n3. **Stopping at package boundaries.** For a design system's own deep dependencies, treat their internals as opaque. This changes answers and could give wrong types, so I'd only consider it behind an explicit option.\n4. **`lib` and DOM typings.** The DOM declarations are among the largest single files. React's types reference them, so it likely isn't a practical cut.\n\n**How to know which pays.** The checker records every file it read or looked up, because the kept answer needs that list. So we can already count, per project, how many files and bytes come from `@types/*`, TypeScript's libs, `node_modules` dependencies and the project itself. That is a measurement, not a guess.\n\nI added three experiments to the profiling worker's list, run on Chakra and shadcn: the loaded files and bytes by origin, the memory with a minimal `types` list, and the memory with the component files split into batches. They report as measured savings in `memory-profile.md`. After the worker reports I'll tell you which lever pays and what it costs."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2681,"cache_read_input_tokens":844070,"output_tokens":2494,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1042},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2681,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2494,"cache_read_input_tokens":844070,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2681,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2681},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"cbc0c0ca-cbbc-4351-8843-f3d915bd835a","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch74HuFdM868YVKQA26mi","requestRef":"f483b1e2-f840-4e35-8920-45cd26ab49b1","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"621bb5a1-a00d-49ea-b87b-cf83c4f867f9","timestamp":"2026-11-16T18:11:19.780Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/caliper/.claude/worktrees/agent-abdd35c983e9e5db8","sessionId":"ad4b3461-bb6f-49f0-bb4d-997d9b8887ba","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"mf-t4","slug":"hazy-conjuring-volcano"}
{"parentUuid":"621bb5a1-a00d-49ea-b87b-cf83c4f867f9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch74HucTi8dWQCso9vxqP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F781tonUoERsAXg7rdWqE5","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"ae3f305f76d56d9f5","summary":"add three checker-loading experiments","message":"Add to the profile (same deliverable, same cap; measured, scratch only): the user asks 'is there a way to narrow down what the checker loads for us?'. Context: the program is already rooted only at the files of the exported components (crates/semantic/src/lib.rs ~251, rpc.rs `open_programs`); default options at rpc.rs:873 are { jsx, module, moduleResolution, target, strict, skipLibCheck, noEmit } with NO `types` and no `lib` list. Experiments: (E1) the load set by origin: using the recorded reads (the kept-answer `reads` list: each file read or looked for; see lib.rs ~187-197 and checker.rs) count files and bytes by origin for Chakra, shadcn and Mantine: project source, node_modules/@types/* per package, TypeScript lib files (which ones: lib.dom.d.ts size), other node_modules declarations (top 10 packages by bytes), and `looked for but missing`. (E2) `types`: run the checker with `types` set to a minimal list (e.g. [\"react\"]) versus unset on the same projects (scratch change to the default options JSON or a tsconfig passed via the existing tsconfig option; do not commit) and report peak RSS, time, loaded files/bytes, and whether any prop type in the output changes (diff canonical JSON of the whole project: count changed props and show 3 examples). (E3) batching: split the root files into 2, 4 and 8 sequential batches (scratch: a driver that calls the existing request path per batch if feasible without large changes; if it needs a real code change in rpc/lib.rs, make it in your scratch copy only and keep it under ~60 lines) and report peak RSS and total time per batch count, and that outputs are identica